  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nLow pressure system has push east, although the trailing cold\nfront continues to bring low clouds and some breezy northwest\nwinds. With these low clouds have also been flurries and pockets\nof mist. Low clouds, flurries, and mist could linger into this\nevening then a surface high and upper level ridging will start\nto diminish clouds and winds. Clearing to partially clearing\nskies and light winds are then expected for tonight. Perhaps\nsome patchy fog is possible, although increasing dry air will\nlimit this threat. Lows tonight will be a bit cooler and\ngenerally near 10 degrees, provided clouds clear as forecast.\nSurface high moves across the state on Monday, bringing a mix of\nclear skies and high clouds. Light and variable winds will\nbecome southerly as the high moves east. Dry conditions can also\nbe expected, along with cooler lows near 30 degrees. A warming\nsouthwest flow aloft could bring some increased mid to high\nclouds Monday night, which will limit the fog threat. Lows\nMonday night will generally be in the teens.\n\nGuidance showing a broad clipper system moving through Tuesday\nand Wednesday. NBM maintains a dry forecast, likely given the\ndry air associated with this system. The strength of this wave\nmay be enough to create some isolated rain or snow showers. For\nnow confidence was not high enough to deviate from NBM, and\nmaintained a dry forecast. Otherwise a steady southerly flow at\nthe surface will bring slightly warmer and near normal\ntemperatures in the 30s through mid week. Lows are forecast in\nthe teens and 20s. A slight increase in RH combined with the\nmentioned southerly flow could provide for some fog each night,\nalthough confidence was not high enough to include at this time.\n\nThanksgiving through the upcoming weekend, zonal to northwest\nflow will provide for some warming temperatures during this time\nperiod. How warm will depend on placement of the western ridge.\nForecast has highs in the 40s Thanksgiving Day and Friday,\nbecoming slightly cooler in northwest flow this weekend. Lows\nwill generally be in the 20s. Overall there remains high\nconfidence in a mainly dry day for Thanksgiving. As the zonal\nflow become more northwest, there could be weak disturbances\nthat bring low chances for rain and snow. Right now this\ntransition could occur on Friday. Forecast remains dry, although\nworth monitoring going forward. This potential wave on Friday\ncould also bring some breezy to windy westerly winds, especially\nacross the west. As mentioned slightly cooler more near normal\ntemperatures are then possible for the upcoming weekend, with\nsome lingering cloud cover possible. This is dependent on where\nthe frontal boundary stalls in northwest flow and placement of \nthe ridge to the west. Overall those with travel plans will\nstill want to monitor the latest forecast for any adverse\nchanges to the timing of these potential waves.",
